WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.455
One classic coding round sits in most loops.

2
00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:04.643
It is a filter.

3
00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:08.240
A great design round does not save a failed coding round.

4
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.865
The good news: most questions come from a small set of patterns.

5
00:00:12.885 --> 00:00:15.075
First: read the input size.

6
00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:18.185
The size tells you the speed the interviewer wants.

7
00:00:18.205 --> 00:00:20.760
Up to a thousand items, two loops are fine.

8
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.335
Up to a million, you need one pass, or a sort.

9
00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:26.645
Second: solve it in one pass.

10
00:00:26.665 --> 00:00:28.732
A hash map remembers what you saw.

11
00:00:28.752 --> 00:00:31.180
So you never search the array again.

12
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.740
Two pointers walk in from both ends of a sorted array.

13
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.690
A sliding window grows and shrinks over the array.

14
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.115
Use it for the longest or the shortest part.

15
00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:43.550
Third: a few more patterns to know.

16
00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.463
A stack finds the nearest match.

17
00:00:45.483 --> 00:00:46.675
A heap keeps the top k.

18
00:00:46.695 --> 00:00:49.335
Binary search cuts the space in half.

19
00:00:49.355 --> 00:00:52.223
For tasks that depend on other tasks, sort the graph.

20
00:00:52.243 --> 00:00:54.935
That is topological sort.

21
00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:57.820
Last: a method for the 45 minutes.

22
00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.020
Restate the problem.

23
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.695
Give a simple solution first.

24
00:01:00.715 --> 00:01:01.582
Improve it.

25
00:01:01.602 --> 00:01:04.545
Then test with a small example.

26
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:06.755
Quick recap of the coding round.

27
00:01:06.775 --> 00:01:07.742
Read the size.

28
00:01:07.762 --> 00:01:08.880
Know the patterns.

29
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.640
And always say the simple solution first.

30
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.760
Now start the first lesson below.
